#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
Build an OTA artefact from a contents YAML file.
Usage: otamaker [<options>] <contents file> [<outfile>]

Each payload is read from the file named by its 'source:' key, or by a comment
on its 'name:' line (the older form), or failing both by its name. Paths are
relative to the current directory, or to -C <dir>. 'source:'
is dropped from the copy of the contents in the artefact. If SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH
is set in the environment, the archive members are given that mtime.

-t builds a transitional artefact, which applicators can install either as a
manifest (format 2) artefact or, if they predate manifests, as a legacy
artefact. -m builds a manifest-only artefact, and -l a legacy one. The default
is currently -t.

Manifests can be signed with ed25519 keys (-k), e.g. made with:
  openssl genpkey -algorithm ed25519 -out key.pem
A device which has a trusted keys file (/etc/rpi-connect/rpi-ota-trusted-keys)
only accepts artefacts signed by one of its keys. --print-pubkey prints the
line to add to that file for a key.

To sign with a key which otamaker can't use directly (e.g. in an HSM), run
otamaker with --signed-data to write the data to be signed, sign it, and run
otamaker again with the same contents and payloads, adding -s with a file
holding the line:
  ed25519 <key id> <base64 signature>
where the key id is as printed by --print-pubkey.
"""

